# Every settled layer under a composed layer, with its remainder carried up. One row per (composed
# layer, settled layer) pair, the factors along the path already multiplied in.
#
# The walk taken as a join
#   The stopping rule is the whole content. A fusion's remainder is defined level by level through
#   its parts, and the natural form needs a sum in the recursive step, which SQL forbids. So the
#   levels are flattened: write each level in terms of the one below and the layers in between drop
#   out, leaving one sum with the factors multiplied along each path. A running product is one value
#   per row and is allowed in a recursive step, so composition/descent.sqlc's walk carries it and
#   this is a join.
#
# The product along a path needs there to be one path
#   "The factors multiplied along each path" is a single number per layer only where each layer is
#   reached one way. Which it is, is a measurement and not a property of multiplication: it is the
#   layer graph having no loops, which rank/cycle_space.sqlc reports and checks/jagged_layer
#   enforces. Two paths to one layer give two products and the arithmetic chooses neither, which is
#   why the guard below declines rather than picking one.
#
# It stops at the first settled layer, not at the bottom
#   That is not an optimisation. A settled layer's nameplate and demand were never scaled by one
#   factor that is a range, so subtracting them is legitimate, and its filed totals are a claim its
#   composer stands behind. Go down past one and those totals are bypassed, along with every
#   correction that composer already applied, which then has to be rebuilt from below and can fail
#   for reasons the filed figure had already settled. `merge-group-composition/compute-pt` is that
#   case: it files a demand, its own conversion is a single value, and one of its eliminations is
#   unsized. Stopping there reads a figure; running past it reaches a correction nobody sized.
#
# A layer at the bottom is the special case
#   composition/unsettled.sqlc is the condition, and a layer at the bottom meets it trivially by
#   having no parts at all. It is a special case of where the walk stops, not the rule.
#
# The same rule about ends, with the product along the path as the factor
#   For a positive product the bounds are the least and the greatest of the two ends on each side,
#   which comes to the bound-by-bound form when the layer's remainder is not below zero and reverses
#   when it is.
#
# `NOT w.is_cycle` is a guard, not a detector
#   It keeps a looped path out of the arithmetic and reports nothing about one. A document whose
#   parts loop has no order to evaluate them in at all, and `checks/layers_move_together` is what
#   says so; this relation simply declines to return a number for it.
-- composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc less the nodes composition/unsettled.sqlc names,
-- against layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc at the node the walk stops on.
SELECT w.root_filing AS composition, w.root_layer AS composed_layer,
       w.filing AS node_filing, w.layer AS node_layer, w.depth,
       w.factor_low, w.factor_mode, w.factor_high,
       least(   r.r_low  * w.factor_low, r.r_low  * w.factor_high) AS r_low,
       r.r_mode * w.factor_mode                                    AS r_mode,
       greatest(r.r_high * w.factor_low, r.r_high * w.factor_high) AS r_high,
       (w.factor_low IS DISTINCT FROM w.factor_high)               AS spread_factor
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc)
) w
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
) r ON r.filing = w.filing AND r.layer = w.layer
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/unsettled.sqlc)
) o ON o.filing = w.filing AND o.layer = w.layer
WHERE w.usable
  AND o.filing IS NULL
